{"text": "\u2014FoR-SRORER ULTRA 8 January 1946\n\nSPECIAL REPORT\n\nFrom: STANCICC Subcommittee on Intelligence and Security.\nTo: STANCICC.\n\nSubj: Security Regulations,\n\nlis FACTS.\n_ The STANCICC Subcommittee on Intelligence and Security was\n\ndirected by a memorandum, dated 17 December 195,, from the STANCICC\n\nSecre*ariat to this subcommittee, to consider the London SIGINT\n\nBoard's \"Explanatory Instructions and Regulations Concerning the\nHandling of Signal Intelligence\" (short title, IRSIG) and offer\nrecommendations thereon. The subcommittee met on 27 December 1945\n\nfor this purpose,\n\n_ Be CONCLUSIONS.\n\nThe London SIGINT Board has indicated certain paragraphs of\nIRSIG upon which they think it essential that there be agreement\nbetween the two countries, The subcommittee discussed these para-\ngraphs and offers the faluning comments:\nk. The U,S. Army is in agreement with the British on the use\nof the words \"Signal Intelligence\", but the U.S. Navy prefers\nthe words \"Communication Intelligence\".\n\u00e9& It is the understanding of the subcommittee that, in\neffect, the word 'MAUVE\u201d indicates in the main that intelli-\ngence which has heretofore been called \"diplomatic't in the\n\u2018United States, and that, in effect, the word \"CREAM\" indicates\n\nwhat has in the main been called \"military\". The subcommittee\n\n\u00e9-SS\n\nc\n\nse OF\n\nDeclassified and approved for release by NSA on 04-08-2010 pursuant to E.O. 12958, as\n\namended. ST56834\n\n(fe)",
